Omar's persistent floating assistant

After onboarding, Omar should shrink into a small floating 2D character near a screen corner.

Important behavior:

- Keep him visible on main screens, unless the user disables him.
- Do not cover important controls, text, navigation, or input fields.
- Respect screen insets and the software keyboard.
- Use a consistent corner position.
- Keep the character small and lightweight.
- Avoid constant movement or distracting animations.
- A subtle idle pose is sufficient.
- Avoid automatically opening help dialogs.

When the user taps Omar, open a small, elegant help panel or bottom sheet.

The panel should offer contextual actions:

- "Explain this screen."
- "How does this feature work?"
- "Where can I find something?"
- "Open the help guide."
- "Hide Omar temporarily."

Omar's help must depend on the current screen. For example, on the flashcard screen, explain how to review cards; on the subjects screen, explain how to organize courses.

Implement contextual help using predefined, localized text and deterministic navigation rules. The current screen identifier should select the relevant explanation.

This is NOT an AI chatbot.

Do not call an AI API, send personal study data to a server, or pretend Omar understands free-form questions.

Omar may display brief contextual tips and predefined responses in both Arabic and French.

Allow the user to disable or re-enable Omar in settings.

Optional encouragement

Use a small library of predefined encouraging messages.

Show them sparingly, such as after finishing a study session or when opening rescue mode.

Never interrupt an active study session with unsolicited dialogue.

Avoid guilt, shame, romantic pressure, or manipulative messaging.

